2 CONT Vähäkyrö rippikirja 1865-1872 (MKO152-167 I Aa:15)  Sivu 227 Merikart by, Skarra ; SSHY http://www.sukuhistoria.fi/sshy/sivut/jasenille/paikat.php?bid=27561&pnum=221 / Viitattu 22.11.2022
2 CONT
2 CONT Vähäkyrö rippikirja 1873-1879 (MKO168-185 I Aa:16)  Sivu 196 (Merikart by) No 2 Sauso ; SSHY http://www.sukuhistoria.fi/sshy/sivut/jasenille/paikat.php?bid=27562&pnum=196 / Viitattu 23.11.2022
2 CONT
2 CONT Merikaarto, Tullenberg
2 CONT Vähäkyrö rippikirja 1880-1892 (MKO186-200 I Aa:17)  Sivu 357 Merikaaron kylä, Sauso ; SSHY http://www.sukuhistoria.fi/sshy/sivut/jasenille/paikat.php?bid=27563&pnum=314 / Viitattu 23.11.2022
2 CONT
2 CONT Vähäkyrö rippikirja 1893-1902 (MKO214-229 I Aa:19)  Sivu 401 Merikaarton kylä, Sauso ; SSHY http://www.sukuhistoria.fi/sshy/sivut/jasenille/paikat.php?bid=27566&pnum=351 / Viitattu 23.11.2022
2 CONT
2 CONT Merikaarronhistoria
2 CONT https://merikaarronhistoria.blogspot.com/p/koulun-historia.html
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Liisa Tuomaantytär Alm was the second wife of Mikko Mikonpoika Tullenberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Liisa was born in Merikaarro, Vähäkyrö, on March 24, 1838. His parents were Tuomas Israelinpoika Alm (b. 13.5.1804, d. 1.2.1870) and Maria Henrikintytär Trofast (b. 20.10.1807, d. 29.12.1853).
2 CONT
2 CONT The family can be found in Vähäkyrö's register book 1851-1858
2 CONT
2 CONT Liisa apparently became a maid between 1855 and 1857, when we can find communion notes from her in Rövarla village at Vanhala no 2 5/16 mantal farm. From there, on 3/1858, she moved to Järviskylä, Uusitalo 5/24, instead of mantal,
2 CONC  as a maid. From there, he moved with his parents to the same farm in Sauso, Merikaarro (communion entries 1860 and 1861). The next phase of his life is missing. However, in 1863, on April 18, she was married to Anders Carlsson Su
2 CONC ndell, born in Vöyri on August 29, 1843.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1st spouse: married 18.4.1863, Anders Carlsson Sundell b. 29.8.1843 Vöyri, Rökiö
2 CONT
2 CONT as the title in the announcements and marriage information, the farmhand's son Anders Karlsson Sundell and Piika Lisa Tuomaantytär Alm. Anders will be 20 years old and Liisa 25 years old when they get married.
2 CONT Anders Carlsson Sundell was born in Vöyri and moved to Vähänkyrö. He can be found in Vähäkyrö's scribe book 1858-1864 in the same scribe book magazine as Mikko Tullenberg's family. He is recorded as an apprentice, so apparently he
2 CONC  was a shoemaker's apprentice with Mikko Tullenberg. Anders has communion records at this address in 1860 and 1861.
2 CONT
2 CONT   In the same year that Liisa and Anders got married, Anders moved from Vöyri to Laihia in July (27.7.2863). So Anders was still enrolled in Vöyri after getting married and Liisa was enrolled in Vähäsäkyrö. Liisa moved from Vähäst
2 CONC äkyrö to Laihia in August 1863 (5 August 1863)
2 CONT
2 CONT They moved to a village called Radde, to a house in Mäenpää. They will have a son in September, so Liisa will be late in her pregnancy when she moves to Laihia. The dates also reveal that the child is born only 5 months after the 
2 CONC wedding.; Adolf Antinpoika Sundell b. 11.9.1863. When Adolf was born, it is mentioned that his father is a shoemaker. He has been Mikko Tullenbeg's shoemaker's apprentice before.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shoemaker Anders Sundelle and his family moved from Laihia back to Vähänkyrö after less than a year on 4.7.1864, where they are not recorded as having moved in until 15.11.1864. In Vähäkyrö, they settle in Skarra's house in Merika
2 CONC arto.
2 CONT
2 CONT There, their next child was born in less than a couple of years, i.e. daughter Rosa Sofia Andersdotter Sundell (b.26.3.1866 Vähäkyrö, Merikaarto, Skarra). After a couple of years, a third child is born, a boy named Karl (b. 20.10.
2 CONC 1868 Vähäkyrö, Merikaarto, Skarra.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter Rosa Sofia dies one month after Karl's birth of throat infection at the age of 2 years and 7 months (d. 9 November 1868).
2 CONT A couple of years later in 1870, in October, Liisa's husband Anders died of pneumonia at the age of 27 (d. 7 October 1870) and a couple of days later son Karl also died of pneumonia, because the church register has the entry Do wh
2 CONC ich means the same as above. Karl was 11 months old when he died (d 9 October 1870). Liisa became a widow at the age of 32 and buried her husband and youngest child at the same time. He was left with sole custody of his 7-year-old
2 CONC  son Adolf. As a widow, Liisa still lives in Skarra's house in Merikaarro with her son Adolf. Communion entries in the years 1873-1875. Skarra's house is right next to Mäenpää's house, at least the order of the register informatio
2 CONC n is sequential. Tullenberg's shoemaker's family lives in Mänepää's house.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1877, i.e. after about 7 years, he moved to Sauso's house No 2 5/16m in Merikaarro. The shoemaker Tullenberg's family also moved to Sauso's group of houses a year earlier, so they have lived in the same village and as neighbors
2 CONC  for years. When Maria Tullenberg dies in April 1878, Mikko becomes a widow, and already in December of the same year, Liisa is married to Mikko.
2 CONT
2 CONT Liisa has been a widow for 7 years and Mikko for 8 months. The wedding day is 1 December 1878 and Liisa is 40 years old and Mikko 59 years old at that time. In the marriage records, Liisa is said to be inh.enk, i.e. she was a self
2 CONC -employed widow. However, according to earlier information, Anders was a shoemaker, just like his new spouse Mikko. Liisa's ex-husband had been a shoemaker's apprentice at Miko before getting married.
2 CONT
2 CONT Liisa and Adolf move in with Miko and her children Juhan 14, Kalle 8 and Maria Mathilda 5. with Sauso No2 in the house. Mikko's children go out into the world gradually. Serafia has probably started working as a maid even before L
2 CONC iisa came, after the death of mother Maria. Similarly, Jaakko left in August 1878, but returned to Sausso, from where he moved to Seinäjoki in 1882. Juho went to the reng in 1879-1880 and then to America, so Kalle and Maria Mathil
2 CONC da, Miko's children, grew up in the family.
2 CONT Seven years after the wedding, a son is born to Miko and Liisa: Leonard Mikelsson Tullenberg b. 14.2.1885 Vähäkyrö, Merikaarto, Sauso. Liisa was 47 years old and Mikko 56 years old when Leonard was born. At the time, Mikko's child
2 CONC ren Kalle was 16 years old and Maria was 12 years old. Liisa's son Adolf got married the same year in January and moved away. In 1891, stepson Kalle moved to Lappajärvi and stepdaughter Maria Mathilda became a maid.
2 CONT
2 CONT Liisa works as a teacher at Merikaarro school. There isn't really a school, because the location of the school was repeatedly a problem. The minutes of the village meeting on February 20, 1895 reveal that the construction had been
2 CONC  abandoned and the 20 logs already reserved for the project were sold to Juho Liuko for 20 FIM 40 pence. On August 13, 1896, we again had to look for a building to rent or buy. Apparently this time, Liisa Tullenberg took over to r
2 CONC un the school in her own room, according to the Merikaarro school history. Liisa had attended the pre-seminar and taught in her own cabin, which was on the hill behind the present-day Vennerholmi on the municipal water line, facin
2 CONC g the river. Birch grows in the ruins of the old fireplace.
2 CONT
2 CONT Liisa and Mikko then live with their son Leonard, but Leonard dies at the age of 13 years and 9 months on 19 November 1898 in Vähäkyrö, Merikaarto. The cause of death is unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Liisa becomes a widow for the second time in 1901, when her husband Mikko dies on October 11, 1901 at the age of 71. Liisa was 63 years old at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the history of the Merikaarro school, it is mentioned that in 1906 Liisa received a salary of 132 marks for her teaching work, of which 84 marks came from the children's parents.
2 CONT
2 CONT at the Tullenberg School
2 CONT HANNA SEPPÄLÄ tells about life at TULLENERGI SCHOOL
2 CONT "Grandma ran a small school. Liisa Tullenberg ran a small school. Väinö goes there and I cried a lot. Once in the afternoon Väinö came to eat and I cried again. My mother says that she can't stand that park anymore and took me the
2 CONC re. My grandmother says that it's bad when the others are already 3 weeks ahead.When the other 3 weeks had passed, I had caught up with the others.
2 CONT We were at Kinker. Grandma says to read aloud to hear everything. That was Pastor Saarinen. I had turned 6 and was counting down to go. Your mother was embarrassed, but Grandma told you to speak loudly.
2 CONT There was no break at school, and when..., it wasn't too busy there, there was business around the corner of Mäenpää's rihi. We went home to eat, there wasn't even a cow. Little school was allowed until public school came, I was p
2 CONC robably 2 ½ years old. The books were a priest's book, a catechism, Aapises was all the letters, we met, there was a chalkboard, a Sunday school hymn book, a hymn book.
2 CONT They didn't teach math there. .Wouldn't it be so that 2 FIM tuition fees in the fall, 3 FIM. in spring. Whoever had a bottle of milk will give the rest to grandma. Wheat was not cultivated. Old grandmother Liisa had to go to the m
2 CONC unicipal hospital. Has died in Vuorela, Saarensivun, as a nurse for the municipality. It also had a whip, metal, hit the desk. 5-6 children sat next to each other at the desk. "Oh, you lazy turkey" might be what grandma said. Ther
2 CONC e were probably equally boys and girls. There was one small room with a fireplace. Maybe it was 8 when we left in the morning, and it was 4. Grandma walked behind with an iron whip."
2 CONT -----------------------------
2 CONT Merikaarro's history says that in her old days, Liisa was very unreserved. The students have said that Liisa collected the leftovers from the students' snacks and the rest of the milk from the bottles for herself. In the minutes o
2 CONC f the village of Merikaarro, there is a note that in September 1906, a grant was granted from the village's funds for the amount needed to repair teacher Liisa Tullenberg's room. The costs seem to have accumulated to 34 marks and 
2 CONC 10 pennies. In July 1908, it was decided to put a new wall in the hut and purchase the materials with the village's funds. The mason's salary was left to the owner. In January 1911, the board voluntarily decided to collect enough 
2 CONC wood for the school from house to house in the village. It is uncertain whether it was still a Tullenerg school. The location of the Tullenberg school is pictured in the history of the Merikaarro school. According to the previousl
2 CONC y quoted text, Liisa Tullenberg had to be cared for by the municipality when she was old and would have died in Saarensivun's Vuorela. The estimated time of death is >1911
