3 CTRY Norway
1 DEAT
2 DATE 15 APR 1939
2 ADDR
3 CITY Paullina
3 STAE Iowa
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sam Roseland Answers Call  Death Comes After Long Illness at His Home Here
2 CONT
2 CONT Sam Roseland passed away Saturday evening at his home here after a long illness of many months. Funeral services were held Tuesday afternoon from the First Presbyterian church, with Rev. Frielie E. Connaway officiating. Interment 
2 CONC was made in the Prairie View Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Roseland was well known in this community, having lived here for many years. He was for a long period employed as a janitor of the Paullina Public Schools, and recent years he was engaged as a salesman for stock minerals.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1871 Samuel Roseland 1939
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Roseland was born April 3, 1871 in Roseland, Norway, and passed away at his home in Paullina on April 15, 1939.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of fourteen, he came to America, and after a few years of work in other places, he came to the Paullina Community, where he has resided since.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was united in marriage in 1895 to Della G. Hodgdon at Paullina, Iowa. To this union were born four sons and three daughters. Paul of Lost Angeles, Calif.; John of Clear Lake, Iowa; Charles of Wood River, Ill.; George of Paullin
2 CONC a; Mrs. Jennings Adams, St. Louis, Missouri, Ada Roseland, Paullina, Iowa; and Mrs. John Lange, Jr. of Paullina, Iowa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Besides his wife and children Mr. Roseland is survived by two sisters and two brothers. Ida Chamness of West Branch, Iowa; Mrs. Howard Outland, LeGrand, Iowa; Thore T. Roseland, San Antonio, Texas, and Tobias Roseland of Roseland,
2 CONC  Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are also nine grandchildren, Jane and Richard Adams, Gayle and Wanda Roseland, Bobby Jack and Patty Roseland, Jimmie Roseland, and Richard Charles Roseland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Roseland, with his wife, united with the Presbyterian church of Paullina in 1912, and continued in that fellowship until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT His long residence and labors in Paullina and vicinity have made him many friends and acquaintances, who join with the family in sorrow for his suffering and his passing. The Times extends its sympathy to the bereaved family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Relatives and friends from a distance who attended the funeral services were: Mr. and Mrs. J.M. Adams of St. Louis, Mo.; Mr Charles Roseland of Wood River, Ill; John Roseland of Clear Lake, Iowa; Mrs. Ida Chamness of West Branch, 
2 CONC Ia.; Mr. and Mrs. Harry Hodgdon, Mr. and Mrs. Marvin Kisch and Mrs. Julia Young of sioux Falls, S.D.; Mrs. Ada Hodgdon of Charles City; Mrs. Beda Kastman, Miss Alma Kastman and Art Kastman of Omah, Nebr; Mrs. G. Masonholder and so
2 CONC n Henry and Delmar Laue of Sioux City, Iowa; Mrs. John Ernest, Mrs. J.H. Ernest, Mrs. John Stecker, Misses Alice Masonholder, Angela Shelketon, Alic Cooper, Pearl Manz, Frances Buckley, Leila Johnson and Zielman of Akron, Iowa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Paullina Times, Paullina, Iowa, April 20, 1939, p1
2 CONT
2 CONT Sam owned a billiards hall in Paullina Iowa. The family home was on the floor above it for a time. He became sheriff of Paullina. Source; his son John Tobias Roseland.
